WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
(music)

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:13.540
My name is Mark Zoack. I'm a professor
of geophysics at Stanford University and

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:17.369
the director of the Stanford Natural
Gas Initiative. I have three degrees in

00:00:17.369 --> 00:00:22.040
geophysics and undergraduate degree from the University of Arizona and a Masters

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:27.360
and PhD from Stanford University. I did
a postdoctoral fellowship at the U.S.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:31.910
Geological Survey. I originally came to
Stanford to complete my graduate

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:36.180
education. I had an undergraduate degree, I worked in the oil industry for two

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.370
years and I realized that there was an
awful lot I didn't know with just a

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:45.649
bachelor's degree and, uh, when I applied to graduate schools Stanford was the best

00:00:45.649 --> 00:00:51.420
school that would take me and so it was
an obvious decision. In my research group

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:57.079
we do a variety of things. We study
reservoir geomechanics; we take data that

00:00:57.079 --> 00:01:01.300
comes from oil and gas development
activities and we try to figure out the

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:05.630
subsurface. We take core samples from
these reservoirs and try to understand

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:10.750
them under realistic conditions and we
do regional studies mostly trying to

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:14.380
understand the forces that are in the
crust how they cause earthquakes how

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:17.000
they affect their large-scale geologic
processes.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.180
(music)

